Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — use your legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security records (the name on your Social Security card) whenever a form is asking for your SSN. Mismatches can cause verification problems, delays, or rejected/flagged records.

Why it matters
- Many agencies (SSA, IRS, employers, banks) match your SSN to the name on Social Security Administration records. If the name on the form doesn’t match SSA records, the system may not be able to verify your SSN or credit wages/tax information correctly.
- Exactness matters most for government/tax/benefit uses (W‑2s, tax returns, SSA forms, passport/ID verifications). For commercial forms (some merchant accounts, newsletters), small variations may be tolerated, but it can still cause problems.

What “match” usually means
- Exact legal name is best: first, middle (or middle initial), last, and any suffix (Jr., Sr., III) should be the same as your SSA record.
- Minor punctuation/hyphenation or use of a middle initial vs full middle name is often tolerated by some systems, but not guaranteed.
- Nicknames (Bill vs William), married vs maiden names, or omitted suffixes can cause mismatches if those are different from your SSA record.

If you’re unsure what SSA has on file
- Check your Social Security card or your SSA account online (ssa.gov/myaccount).
- If the SSA record is wrong or you’ve legally changed your name, update SSA with the required proof (ID, marriage certificate, court order).

If a form only requests the last four digits
- Some places accept just the last four for low‑risk verification, but they still often expect the name you give to match records. Using the legal name remains the safest option.

Security note
- Avoid posting or sharing your full SSN in public or insecure channels. If you quoted it here for context, consider it sensitive and be careful where you share it.

If you want, you can:
- Tell me how your name appears on the document (don’t include your SSN) and how it appears on your Social Security card, and I’ll tell you whether that difference is likely to be a problem.
- I can walk you through checking or updating your SSA record.

