Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — use the exact legal name that’s on your Social Security record whenever you’re asked for your SSN. Mismatches can cause verification problems.

Why it matters
- Agencies (SSA, IRS, employers for W‑2s/I‑9) match your SSN to the name on file. If the spelling differs, your record may not be found or earnings may not be credited correctly, and you could get follow‑up requests or delay.
- Banks, background‑check companies, and credit bureaus often require the legal name; some will accept minor differences but others will not.
- Small formatting differences (hyphens, periods) are usually fine; differences in spelling, order, missing/extra suffixes (Jr./III), or different last names (marriage/divorce) are more likely to cause problems.

Practical steps
1. Verify the exact name SSA has on file:
   - Check your Social Security card or sign in to your “my Social Security” account online.
2. If the name on the form is wrong, ask the requester whether they need the name exactly as SSA has it. If they do, give them the SSA name or ask to correct the form.
3. If your SSA record is wrong or you legally changed your name, update SSA (Form SS‑5 or online where available) and provide legal documentation (marriage certificate, court order, etc.).
4. If you can’t change the form, include both names if there’s a field for “also known as” or add a brief note with supporting ID, and ask the organization what they accept.
5. Don’t send your full SSN in unsecured messages — only give it where absolutely necessary and via secure channels.

One small note: you wrote “XXX-XX-2345 (last four: 7890).” Those two last-four numbers don’t match — make sure you use the correct last four when verifying records. 

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to HR/the organization asking whether the SSA name is required; or
- Help you word the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card for the form.

