I won’t repeat your SSN here for privacy, but I can explain how name matching usually works and what to do if the spelling on your document doesn’t exactly match the name the Social Security Administration has on file.

Key points
- For official SSN verification (SSA, IRS, employers), the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security card (your SSA record). Exact spelling matters because mismatches can trigger verification problems, wage-reporting errors, delays in benefits, or required follow-up.
- Minor differences often tolerated: punctuation (periods, commas), hyphens, middle initials vs. full middle name, or extra spaces are often OK. But substantive differences (misspelled last name, wrong given name, omitted legal middle name when SSA uses it, or using a nickname instead of your legal first name) can cause problems.
- For banks and many businesses: they will usually accept small variations but may ask for supporting ID if something looks different.
- For passports, driver’s licenses, or other ID required by the form: name should match the ID you present. If that ID differs from SSA records, expect additional verification.

What to do if the name doesn’t match
1. Check your SSA record: Use the exact name that appears on your Social Security card. If you don’t remember it, you can check with SSA (in person or via their online services) — don’t post your SSN publicly.
2. If your legal name changed (marriage, divorce, court order) and SSA doesn’t have the updated name, update SSA first. You’ll need proof (marriage certificate, court order, passport, state ID).
3. If you can’t update SSA immediately, contact the organization asking how they want you to proceed — they may accept additional ID or an “also known as / former name” field.
4. When filling a form, enter your legal name exactly as on your Social Security card when SSN verification is required.
5. If the form has a field for “other names used” or “previous name,” include any alternate names there.

Security tip
- Don’t email your full SSN over unsecured email. If you must provide it, use secure methods the organization specifies.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to HR, a bank, or the agency explaining the name mismatch and asking what they need;
- Or help you word the name exactly how to enter it based on the name on your Social Security card (you can type the name but please don’t type or paste the full SSN). Which would you prefer?

