Short answer: it depends on who’s asking, but when an SSN is used for official identity/benefits/tax purposes the name should match the Social Security Administration (SSA) record as closely as possible — ideally exactly.

A few important points and practical tips:

- I noticed you gave two different “last four” values in your message. Please don’t share your full SSN here — if you meant one particular last-four, just confirm it without posting the whole number.

- When an exact match is required:
  - SSA benefits, updating your SSN card, payroll/employment verification, IRS e‑filing and many bank/KYC processes generally require the name to match the SSA record (the name on your Social Security card). Small mismatches can cause rejections, delays, or requests for documentation.
  - For IRS e‑filing, a name/SSN mismatch will often cause a reject or later notice. Employers who verify new hires using SSA’s database can get a “no match” if the name differs.

- When a looser match is usually acceptable:
  - Informal forms, promotional registrations, or internal non‑regulated documents may accept common variations (nicknames, omitted middle name). Still, using your legal name avoids confusion.

- What “match” means:
  - Include the same legal first/middle/last name, suffixes (Jr./III), and hyphenation/spacing as on your SSA record. Middle initial vs full middle name can matter in some systems.
  - Punctuation differences are less likely to cause problems than name differences, but it’s safest to copy how it appears on your Social Security card.

- If your name has changed:
  - Update your name with SSA (bring marriage certificate, court order, etc.) before using the SSN for new official purposes. You can find SSA appointment/requirements on ssa.gov.
  - Provide supporting documents to the organization requesting the form if they need to reconcile different names.

- If you’re unsure:
  - Check the form for instructions (e.g., “legal name” or “name as shown on Social Security card”).
  - Contact the requesting organization’s helpdesk or HR and ask whether the exact SSA spelling is required or if they accept an alternate form.
  - If you prefer, I can help draft a short message to the organization asking whether an exact SSA match is required.

Would you like help drafting a message to the organization or wording to confirm whether they require the exact SSA name?

