Short answer: yes — in most cases the name on the form should match the name on file with the Social Security Administration (your legal name as shown on your Social Security card). Exact or very close matches avoid verification problems, delays, and requests for corrections.

Details and practical guidance
- Why it matters: many agencies (SSA, IRS, employers, banks, benefits providers) verify the name + SSN against SSA records. A mismatch can trigger a rejection, delay, or additional verification requests.
- What “match” means in practice:
  - Exact legal name (first + middle + last) as on your Social Security card is safest.
  - Minor formatting differences (capitalization, periods, extra spaces) are usually fine.
  - Common variations that can cause problems: different last name (maiden vs married), omitted or different middle name vs initial, transposed names, misspellings, or use of nicknames instead of the legal given name.
  - Diacritics/accents are often dropped in U.S. databases, so those usually aren’t a problem — but spelling must otherwise be the same.
- If your name has changed: update SSA first. If you recently changed your name (marriage, divorce, court order), you should file form SS‑5 with SSA and provide proof. Many organizations will want the SSA record updated before accepting the new name.
- If the form asks for “legal name” vs “preferred name”: use legal name. If it asks for former names or aliases, fill those sections as requested.
- If you’re unsure or the form belongs to a specific agency:
  - Look for instructions on the form (some say “must match Social Security card”).
  - Contact the organization’s helpdesk to ask whether minor differences are acceptable or whether they require documentation.
  - If they need proof, be prepared to provide a copy of your Social Security card or other identity documents.
- Security note: don’t share your full SSN in unsecured email or public messages. Provide only the minimum (often last 4 digits) unless absolutely necessary and secure.

If you tell me what kind of form (tax, employer onboarding/I‑9, bank account, government benefit application, etc.), I can give more specific guidance and sample wording to include on the form or a short message to the organization.

