Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — whenever a form ties your name to your Social Security Number (SSN) for verification, it’s safest to use the exact legal name that is on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Small differences sometimes pass, but mismatches can cause delays or rejections.

Details and practical guidance
- When an exact match is important
  - Social Security Administration (benefits, SSN verification), IRS/tax filings, employer I-9 and payroll, banks/credit checks, and government ID applications. These systems compare the name you give to the name on SSA/IRS records — a mismatch can trigger manual review, payment/refund delays, or requests for proof.
- Common small differences that MAY be tolerated (but are risky)
  - Use/omission of a middle initial, minor punctuation (periods, commas), or common abbreviations (e.g., “Bob” vs “Robert” is riskier). Still: don’t assume — when accuracy matters, use the exact legal name.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order)
  - Update your name with the Social Security Administration first. If SSA records aren’t updated, employers and the IRS will see a mismatch.
- How to check/correct your SSA name
  - Check your record via your my Social Security account at ssa.gov (or call SSA at 1-800-772-1213). To change your name you’ll need original or certified documents (e.g., marriage certificate, court order, U.S. passport, birth certificate) and may need to visit a local SSA office.
- For forms that ask only for last four digits of SSN
  - They often use the last four plus your name and DOB as an identity check. Still use the legal name to avoid complications.
- Privacy note
  - Only provide your full SSN to trusted parties and over secure channels. Sharing just the last four is common for identification purposes — avoid posting or sending the full SSN publicly.

If you want, I can:
