Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: In most cases yes — use the same name spelling that’s on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card) when a form asks for your SSN. Mismatches (even small ones) can cause verification failures, tax e-file rejections, payroll problems, or delays in benefits.

What to know and practical guidance
- Best practice: Enter your name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card (first, middle, last, and any suffixes) when a form requests your SSN.  
- Small formatting differences: Differences in capitalization, punctuation, or presence/absence of a middle initial are often tolerated, but they can still trigger automated mismatches in some systems.  
- Nicknames / informal names: Do not use nicknames — use your legal name unless the card shows otherwise.  
- Hyphens, accents, diacritics: SSA records often omit accents; include hyphens or suffixes only if they appear on your SSA card.  
- If you recently changed your name (marriage, court order): You should update your name with the Social Security Administration first. Many agencies require the SSN and name to match SSA records.

If you discover a mismatch
- Short-term fixes: Provide supporting ID or legal documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license) along with the form or notify the agency that your name differs from the SSA record and you’re working to correct it. Some employers/organizations will accept documentation temporarily.  
- Long-term fix: Update your name with SSA (apply for a corrected SSN card) so future verifications match.

How to update your name with SSA
- Gather proof: identity (U.S. passport or state ID), proof of name change (marriage certificate, court order), and proof of citizenship/immigration status if applicable.  
- Apply: Submit Form SS-5 (online where available or at a local SSA office) or bring documents in person/mail as instructed. Replacement/correction of the card is free. Processing times vary, often about 1–3 weeks after SSA accepts the application (times can vary by office).

Privacy reminder
- Avoid sharing your full SSN in messages or public places. It’s fine to refer to the last four digits when needed.

If you want, tell me:
- Which specific form or organization is asking for your SSN (e.g., W‑4, bank account, mortgage, government benefits), and whether the name on the form differs from your Social Security card — I can give more specific advice or a short note you can include with the form.

