Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — use your legal name as it appears on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Many agencies and automated systems require the name and SSN/last-four to match the SSA record exactly; mismatches can cause delays, rejected filings, payroll problems, or verification failures.

Key details and practical steps
- What “must match” means: For official uses (IRS tax returns, employer wage reporting, SSA verification, many banks and background checks) the name + SSN must match the SSA record. Automated matching systems are strict and may treat even small differences as a mismatch.
- Minor variations: Small differences such as including or omitting a middle initial, a middle name, punctuation (periods, commas), or a hyphen sometimes are tolerated, but there’s no guarantee. If the system is automated, those differences can cause a reject.
- If your name changed (marriage, divorce, court order): update your name with the Social Security Administration first (form SS-5 with proof of identity and proof of the legal name change). Only after SSA updates will other agencies reliably accept the new name with your SSN.
- If you can’t wait for an SSA update: contact the organization receiving the form and ask their policy. They may accept a copy of your legal name-change document (marriage certificate, court order) accompanying the form, or ask you to include your previous/maiden name in a “also known as” field.
- For forms that ask only for the last four of your SSN: those are often used for lower-level verification. Even then, listing your legal name is safer. If the form explicitly only requests last-four, follow its instructions.

How to check and fix things
- Look at your Social Security card (or log in to your SSA online account) to confirm the exact name on file.
- To change your SSA name: complete SSA Form SS-5 and provide required documents (ID, proof of legal name change). See SSA.gov for details and local office instructions.
- For tax issues: the IRS will reject returns if name/SSN don’t match SSA; corrected filings can delay refunds.
- When in doubt, ask the recipient (HR, bank, agency) what they require — getting their guidance prevents rework.

Privacy note
- Don’t share your full SSN in public or insecure messages. You already shared the last four; that’s commonly used for identity checks and is OK to reference if needed, but avoid posting the full number.

If you want, tell me:
- which form you’re filling and how your name appears there (legal/full name versus preferred name), and I’ll give specific wording or next steps you can use.

