Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: for official uses (tax, SSA, payroll, benefits, many banks) your name should match exactly the name on file with the Social Security Administration (the name on your Social Security card). Small informal forms may be more flexible, but when an SSN is involved it’s safest to match SSA records.

What “match exactly” means
- Exact spelling and order of given/family names (including hyphens, spaces, accents if applicable).  
- Middle name vs. middle initial: differences can matter for automated checks.  
- Married/changed names: the SSA file must reflect the new legal name before employers/IRS processing.

Why it matters
- IRS/SSA name+SSN mismatches can cause tax-return delays or rejected filings and create problems with W-2s, benefits, or payroll.  
- Banks and credit checks use automated matching and may freeze or delay accounts when names don’t match the SSN record.

What to do if your name on the document doesn’t match your SSA record
1. Don’t send your full SSN over unsecured email or public chat. Use the form’s secure upload, in-person, or an encrypted channel.  
2. Check your SSA record: create a “my Social Security” account at ssa.gov or visit a local SSA office to confirm the exact name on file.  
3. If the SSA name is wrong or you changed your name, update it with the SSA (Form SS‑5 and required identity/legal documents). Wait until SSA processes the change before submitting documents that rely on the match.  
4. If you can’t change the SSA record right away, contact the organization requesting the SSN and ask what documentation they’ll accept (e.g., marriage certificate plus ID) or whether they can note the name change.  
5. For employers: make sure the name is correct before they submit W-2s. For tax returns, ensure the name matches SSA to avoid IRS processing problems.

A few practical examples
- If your card reads “Elizabeth A. Smith” and the form says “Liz Smith,” automated checks may fail.  
- If you’ve legally changed your last name but didn’t yet update SSA, update SSA first or give the requester supporting legal documentation.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a short message to the organization asking whether minor differences are acceptable and what documents they need.  
- Walk you through what to bring to the SSA office to change your name.

One more note: I see two different last-four values in your message — please double-check the number before submitting, and don’t paste your full SSN into messages here.

