Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: it depends on the purpose of the form, but for anything that will be matched to your Social Security record (payroll, tax forms, benefits, identity verification) it’s safest to use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security card / SSA record. Mismatches can cause delays or verification failures.

Details and practical guidance

- When exact matching matters
  - Employer/payroll, W‑2s, tax filings, benefits, and many background checks usually require the name on the form to match the SSA record. If it doesn’t, your employer or the agency may not be able to verify your SSN and could delay processing or request documentation.
  - Electronic systems (E‑Verify, IRS/TIN matching) can be strict about first/middle/last name and suffixes.

- Small variations that are often tolerated
  - Using a middle initial instead of a full middle name is usually okay, but sometimes triggers a mismatch.
  - Omitted punctuation (periods, commas) and diacritical marks (accents) are often ignored by matching systems.
  - Common nicknames (Bob vs Robert) usually will not match if the SSA record uses the legal name.

- Name elements to pay attention to
  - Suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) should match the SSA record.
  - Hyphenation and spacing (Mary Ann vs Mary-Ann vs MaryAnn) can cause mismatches—use the form that matches SSA.
  - If you have changed your name (marriage, divorce, court order), make sure SSA has been updated.

- If the name on your form won’t match
  - Prefer to use the name exactly as shown on your SSA card when the form requests an SSN.
  - If you cannot use that name (e.g., you’ve legally changed it and not updated SSA), either:
    - Update your SSA record first (see below), or
    - Attach/submit supporting documentation (marriage certificate, court order, passport) and/or a brief explanation to the requester so they can process or advise.
  - Contact the organization asking for the SSN and ask what they require if names differ — some will accept documentation; others require SSA-updated records.

- How to fix/update your SSA name
  - To change the name on your Social Security record or get a new card, submit Form SS‑5 with proof of identity and proof of the legal name change (marriage certificate, divorce decree, court order, or U.S. passport). This is done through the Social Security Administration (local office or by mail) or by creating a my Social Security account to see guidance.
  - After SSA updates, use the updated name on all forms that require an exact SSN/name match.

Privacy reminder
- Avoid sharing your full SSN in email or public messages. Providing only the last four (as you did) is safer.

If you tell me what kind of form this is (employment, bank account, government benefits, tax form, etc.), I can give more specific wording to use or help draft a short explanation or email to the requester.

